From ff6304d0ffe766a308a6d44b0d0c6fe1af2aac92 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Dimitri John Ledkov <dimitri.ledkov@chainguard.dev>
Date: Mon, 14 Sep 2026 20:30:03 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 2/3] Offer DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC from the freestanding
 legacy provider

Register DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC in the legacy module, gated behind a new
provider parameter "enable-des".  It defaults to 0, so the module still
advertises the same seven algorithms as before unless the configuration
section for the provider contains "enable-des = 1" (or the application sets
it with OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(), or an openssl command is given
-provparam legacy:enable-des=1).  The value is read through the core's
get_params upcall in the provider's query callback, using
ossl_prov_ctx_get_bool_param(); the DES entries sit at the front of the
cipher table so the disabled view is the same array starting two entries
in.  A no-des build has no entries there and ignores the parameter.

A module that does not link libcrypto needs its own copy of the DES
primitives whether or not libcrypto exports them, so crypto/des now feeds
liblegacy.a unconditionally rather than only under no-deprecated, and the
TDES EDE3 sources join the single-DES ones there.

The only place these ciphers touch randomness is the
OSSL_CIPHER_PARAM_RANDOM_KEY getter behind EVP_CIPHER_CTX_rand_key(),
which called RAND_priv_bytes_ex() and would have pulled the DRBG into the
module.  Both DES key generators now call ossl_cipher_generic_rand_bytes(),
which uses RAND_priv_bytes_ex() normally and, under LEGACY_MODULE, asks the
core for entropy through the OSSL_FUNC_get_user_entropy /
OSSL_FUNC_get_entropy upcalls -- the same path the FIPS module uses to seed
its DRBGs.  legacyprov.c records those callbacks at init with
ossl_prov_seeding_from_dispatch(), provider_seeding.c is added to
liblegacy.a, and PROV_CIPHER_CTX carries the provider context so the call
can be made.  Encryption and decryption never reach this path.

Property strings are split by a new ALG_NOFIPS() macro.  Only the digests
(MD4, MD5, SHA1) and AES-128-ECB / AES-256-ECB keep
"provider=legacy,fips=yes"; RC4, RC4-40, DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC are
"provider=legacy,fips=no".  An application running with fips=yes default
properties therefore never receives any of those four ciphers from this
module (DES not even with enable-des = 1), and a fips=yes fetch of
DES-EDE3-CBC can only resolve to the FIPS provider's decrypt-only
implementation, never to this copy.

Verified with a no-asm no-quic no-secure-memory build: legacy.so still
depends only on libc and exports only OSSL_provider_init; both DES ciphers
match the evp_test vectors; EVP_CIPHER_CTX_rand_key() from the module
returns fresh odd-parity keys; without enable-des neither DES cipher is
listed or fetchable; "openssl list -propquery fips=yes" shows the three
digests and two AES-ECB ciphers, and "-propquery fips=no" shows exactly
RC4, RC4-40, DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC.

README-LEGACY-FREESTANDING.md documents the parameter, the fips=yes /
fips=no split, the no-rc4 / no-des (and no-md4 / no-md5) configure options
that remove algorithms from the module, known-answer commands for both DES
ciphers, and the fact that evp_test stops a data file at the first cipher
it cannot fetch, so the stock DES data files cannot exercise this module.

