 No KDFs, no key management and no other ciphers or digests are registered.
 
+### Enabling DES
+
+DES-CBC and DES-EDE3-CBC are compiled in but not registered unless the
+provider is told to.  The switch is a provider parameter, `enable-des`,
+which defaults to `0`.  Set it in the provider's configuration section:
+
+    [legacy_sect]
+    activate = 1
+    enable-des = 1
+
+or, for the command-line tools, after loading the provider:
+
+    openssl enc -provider legacy -provparam legacy:enable-des=1 ...
+
+Programmatically, call `OSSL_PROVIDER_add_conf_parameter(prov, "enable-des",
+"1")` on the loaded provider before the first fetch.  The value is read
+through the core's `get_params` each time the cipher list is queried, using
+the same parser as the other providers: `1`, `yes`, `true` and `on` enable,
+`0`, `no`, `false` and `off` disable, anything else is treated as the default.
+Without it, `openssl list` shows seven algorithms from this provider; with
+it, nine.  A `no-des` build has nothing to enable and ignores the parameter.
+
+### Configure options that shrink the set further
+
+The table above is what a default `./Configure` produces.  The usual
+per-algorithm `no-*` options still apply to this module, and remove the
+corresponding entries from it:
+
+| Configure option | Removed from the legacy module |
+|------------------|--------------------------------|
+| `no-rc4`         | RC4, RC4-40                    |
+| `no-des`         | DES-CBC, DES-EDE3-CBC          |
+| `no-md4`         | MD4                            |
+| `no-md5`         | MD5                            |
+
+`no-des` also drops DES-EDE3-CBC from the default and FIPS providers, and
+`no-rc4` drops the RC4-HMAC-MD5 stitched cipher from the default provider, so
+choose these with the whole build in mind, not just this module.  SHA1 and
+AES-ECB cannot be configured out.
